A note on mass collection


Bulk scraping/exporting from LinkedIn and job boards sits in a gray zone — most of these platforms' Terms of Service restrict automated scraping, and LinkedIn actively detects and restricts accounts that bulk-export via extensions. The tools above (Apollo, SalesQL, ContactOut) are built to stay under detection thresholds, but pushing bulk exports aggressively on a single LinkedIn account is the fastest way to get that account restricted — something worth weighing given how central your 15,000+ network is to your differentiation.


One more thing worth flagging: a lot of the "best tools" content currently ranking on Google for this topic is SEO-optimized by one vendor (Pin) promoting itself across nearly every article — so I'd treat any single source's "#1 pick" with some skepticism and trial 2-3 in parallel against a live req to see which actually finds candidates in your specific niches.


The public government data goldmine (specific to your visa-category work)


This is probably the highest-leverage thing here, and almost nobody uses it well:


DOL LCA Disclosure Data — quarterly public filings from the Department of Labor covering every H-1B labor condition application, including employer, job title, wage, and worksite, available free via h1bdata.info, h1bdatawatch.com, or raw bulk CSV from DOL itself. Practical uses: 
Pages

Pull the list of companies filing LCAs for the exact job titles/tech stacks in your open reqs — those companies currently have H1B talent in that skill, some of whom are approaching visa transfer windows or project rolloffs

Note the POC email on an LCA isn't the hiring manager — for that you'd need Section J of the full form, which employers are legally required to provide on request, but the filing employer name alone is enough to reverse-search on LinkedIn 

H1B Data Watch
Cross-reference against your 30 HBITS competitors' names as petitioners — this tells you which of them are actively placing H1B consultants right now, and where
PERM Disclosure Data (green card labor certifications, also DOL, also free) — candidates in active PERM processing are often more open to a lateral move for a faster GC timeline. Same site structure as LCA data.
Build a simple recurring pull: since you already have a working Python/openpyxl pipeline for GitHub, the same approach (script + pandas) works directly on DOL's bulk LCA/PERM CSVs — no scraping ToS issues since it's public government data.
Boolean/X-ray tricks worth layering in


Beyond basic site:linkedin.com/in, the operators that actually widen your net:

filetype:pdf OR filetype:doc "resume" "your-skill" — surfaces resumes hosted on personal sites, university pages, and forgotten Dropbox/Drive public links that never show up in job board search
intitle:"resume" OR intitle:"CV" combined with skill/location terms
inurl:resume -inurl:jobs to filter out job-posting noise
AROUND(n) (Google-specific) — finds two terms within n words of each other, useful for "Java AROUND(3) AWS" to catch resumes phrasing skills differently than your string
Layer NOT terms aggressively to exclude recruiter/staffing-agency chaff: NOT recruiter NOT "talent acquisition" NOT staffing
LinkedIn's own search bar supports AND/OR/NOT (capitalized), quoted phrases, and parentheses, up to roughly 1000 characters — worth building 3-4 saved master strings per skill cluster rather than rewriting each time. 
